      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Number: R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 revision 4\n- Scheme: R49 is a certificate scheme... The scheme field is \"A|B\". OIML Certificates of Conformity are typically scheme A. Actually, for water meters under R49, the certificates are issued under scheme A (basic OIML certificate). Let me think — OIML-CS has scheme A and scheme B. The traditional OIML certificate of conformity is scheme A. This cert from 2012-2015 is a classic OIML certificate, so scheme A.\n- Project number: 13200159\n- Page total: 4\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 18 March 2015\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Address lines: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, the Netherlands\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\n**Manufacturers:** Same as applicant (labeled \"Applicant and Manufacturer\"). Include the same.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\"\n- Type designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- Module designation: The meter is \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\" — module_designation... For water meters, not the load cell module scheme. I'd say null or \"Not applicable\". The cert doesn't explicitly list modules. I'll use null or omit. Actually, maybe \"electronic calculating/indicating device\" — but that's a description, not a module designation per R46/61 style. I'll set module_designation: null (omit).\n- Description: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, model \"WATERFLUX 3070\", class 1 and 2.\" Plus the pattern description: \"Water meter intended for metering cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\"\n\n**Model family:** The cert covers multiple meter sizes DN25 through DN600, with varying accuracy class and flow rates. So model_family with models per DN size. But note some sizes have multiple accuracy class variants (class 1 and 2). The table has rows per DN size × accuracy class × Q3/Q1 ratio variant.\n\nLet me parse the flow table carefully:\n\n```\nMeter size | Accuracy class | Min Q1 | Trans Q2 | Perm Q3 | Over Q4 | Ratio Q3/Q1\nDN25 | 2 | 0.025 | 0.04 | 10 | 12.5 | 400\n     |   | 0.04  | 0.064 | 16 | 20   |\nDN40 | 2 | 0.0625 | 0.1 | 25 | 31.3 | 400\n     |   | 0.1 | 0.16 | 40 | 50 |\nDN50 | 2 | 0.1 | 0.16 | 40 | 50 | 400\n     |   | 0.1575 | 0.252 | 63 | 78.75 |\nDN65 | 2 | 0.1575 | 0.25 | 63 | 78.75 | 400\n     |   | 0.25 | 0.4 | 100 | 125 |\n     | 1 | 0.4 | 0.64 | 100 | 125 | 250\nDN80 | 2 | 0.25 | 0.4 | 100 | 125 | 400\n     |   | 0.4 | 0.64 | 160 | 200 |\n     | 1 | 0.625 | 1 | 100 | 125 | 160\n     |   | 0.64 | 1.02 | 160 | 200 | 250\nDN100 | 2 | 0.4 | 0.64 | 160 | 200 | 400\n      |   | 0.625 | 1 | 250 | 312.5 |\n      | 1 | 1 | 1.6 | 160 | 200 | 160\n      |   | 1 | 1.6 | 250 | 312.5 | 250\nDN125 | 2 | 0.625 | 1 | 250 | 312.5 | 400\n      |   | 1 | 1.6 | 400 | 500 |\n      | 1 | 1.56 | 2.5 | 250 | 312.5 | 160\n      |   | 1.6 | 2.56 | 400 | 500 | 250\nDN150 | 2 | 1 | 1.6 | 400 | 500 | 400\n      |   | 1.575 | 2.52 | 630 | 787.5 |\n      | 1 | 2.5 | 4 | 400 | 500 | 160\n      |   | 2.52 | 4.03 | 630 | 787.5 | 250\nDN200 | 2 | 1.575 | 2.52 | 630 | 787.5 | 400\n      | 1 | 3.94 | 6.3 | 630 | 787.5 | 160\nDN250 | 2 | 2.5 | 4 | 1000 | 1250 | 400\n      | 1 | 6.25 | 10 | 1000 | 1250 | 160\nDN300 | 2 | 4 | 6.4 | 1600 | 2000 | 400\n      | 1 | 10 | 16 | 1600 | 2000 | 160\nDN350 | 1 or 2 | 15.625 | 25 | 2500 | 3125 | 160\nDN400 | 1 or 2 | 25 | 40 | 4000 | 5000 | 160\nDN450 | 1 or 2 | 25 | 40 | 4000 | 5000 | 160\nDN500 | 1 or 2 | 39.375 | 63 | 6300 | 7875 | 160\nDN600 | 1 or 2 | 63 | 100.8 | 6300 | 7875 | 100\n```\n\nWait, for DN65 the class 1 row: \"0.4 | 0.64 | 100 | 125 | 250\". Hmm, ratio 250? Q3/Q1 = 100/0.4 = 250. Yes.\n\nFor DN80 class 1: two rows:\n- Row 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, ratio 160. Q3/Q1 = 100/0.625 = 160. ✓\n- Row 2: 0.64, 1.02, 160, 200, ratio 250. Q3/Q1 = 160/0.64 = 250. ✓\n\nFor DN100 class 1:\n- Row 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, ratio 160. Q3/Q1 = 160/1 = 160 ✓\n- Row 2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, ratio 250. Q3/Q1 = 250/1 = 250 ✓\n\nFor DN125 class 1:\n- Row 1: 1.56, 2.5, 250, 312.5, ratio 160. 250/1.5625 = 160. Hmm, 1.56 vs 1.5625 — 250/1.5625 = 160 exactly. The markdown says 1.56 (probably rounded in the original cert). I'll keep 1.56 as extracted.\n- Row 2: 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, ratio 250. 400/1.6 = 250 ✓\n\nDN150 class 1:\n- Row 1: 2.5, 4, 400, 500, ratio 160. 400/2.5 = 160 ✓\n- Row 2: 2.52, 4.03, 630, 787.5, ratio 250. 630/2.52 = 250 ✓\n\nDN200:\n- Class 2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, ratio 400. 630/1.575 = 400 ✓\n- Class 1: 3.94, 6.3, 630, 787.5, ratio 160. 630/3.9375 = 160. Again 3.94 vs 3.9375.\n\nDN250:\n- Class 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, ratio 400 ✓\n- Class 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, ratio 160 ✓\n\nDN300:\n- Class 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, ratio 400 ✓\n- Class 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, ratio 160 ✓\n\nDN350: 1 or 2, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, ratio 160. 2500/15.625 = 160 ✓\nDN400: 1 or 2, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, ratio 160 ✓\nDN450: same as DN400.\nDN500: 1 or 2, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, ratio 160. 6300/39.375 = 160 ✓\nDN600: 1 or 2, 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, ratio 100. 6300/63 = 100 ✓\n\nNow for the second table (page 4):\n```\nMeter size | Indicating range [m³] | Verification scale interval [m³]\nDN25 | 99.999.999 | 0.00001\nDN40, DN50, DN65 and DN80 | 99.999.999 | 0.0001\nDN100, DN125, DN150, DN200 and DN250 | 99.999.999 | 0.001\nDN300, DN350, DN400, DN450 | 99.999.999 | 0.01\nDN500 and DN600 | 99.999.999 | 0.1\n```\n\nNote \"99.999.999\" is European thousands notation = 99,999,999 m³. Since the rules say decimal comma → decimal point, but this is a thousands separator. 99.999.999 = 99999999. I'll convert to 99999999.\n\nModel variants: How to model this? Options:\n1. Each model = DN size (DN25, DN40, ..., DN600) — 18 sizes.\n2. Each model = DN size + accuracy class + ratio variant — many variants.\n\nThe cert describes \"class 1 and 2\" meters. Each DN size can have multiple rows (different Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4 for class 2 vs 1, and different ratio variants within a class). I think the cleanest approach:\n\n- model_family.models: one per meter size (DN25...DN600), 18 models.\n- model_level characteristics: for attributes that vary per size... but flow rates vary per size AND per class/ratio variant. \n\nHmm. The schema has config_level for attributes varying by configuration. Here the axis is (accuracy_class, ratio_q3_q1). The flow rates vary by both size and class/ratio.\n\nApproach: matrix_tables to preserve the table, plus model_level entries keyed by model_id that encodes size+class+ratio? That could be over-engineering.\n\nSimplest faithful approach:\n- matrix_tables: preserve both tables (flow table and indicating range table).\n- model_family.models: one per DN size (18 models). model_id like \"DN25\".\n- model_level: attributes that are per-size only... verification scale interval varies by size group. Could do model_level with values per model.\n- config_level: flow rates vary by accuracy class (and ratio variant). But the variation is per size too...\n\nActually let me think about how the R137 example handled it: models were per size (G1.6, G2.5) and model_level attributes qmax, qt, qmin with values per model. Here, for sizes with only one row (DN350-DN600), q1/q2/q3/q4 per model directly. For sizes with class 2 and class 1 rows, we could use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" — but the values differ per size too.\n\nAlternative: define models as size+class+ratio combos. E.g.:\n- DN25, class 2, ratio 400, two rows each (the two rows per class within a size correspond to... what?). \n\nHmm wait, why are there two rows per size per class? E.g., DN25 class 2: row 1 (0.025, 0.04, 10, 12.5) and row 2 (0.04, 0.064, 16, 20). Both ratio 400. These are likely two different Q3 options for the same size (e.g., Q3=10 or Q3=16 nominal flow options), both ratio 400. So within DN25 class 2 there are two Q3 options: Q3=10 m³/h and Q3=16 m³/h.\n\nSo the model variants are really size × class × Q3 nominal. That's the way water meter certificates work — meter size DN with different nominal flow Q3.\n\nSo model variants: e.g. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\", \"DN25 class 2 Q3=16\", etc. That's a lot of models (~30+). Hmm.\n\nLet me count rows:\n- DN25: 2\n- DN40: 2\n- DN50: 2\n- DN65: 3 (2 class2 + 1 class1)\n- DN80: 4 (2 class2 + 2 class1)\n- DN100: 4\n- DN125: 4\n- DN150: 4\n- DN200: 2\n- DN250: 2\n- DN300: 2\n- DN350: 1\n- DN400: 1\n- DN450: 1\n- DN500: 1\n- DN600: 1\nTotal: 36 rows.\n\nBut note within DN80 class 1, the two rows have different ratios (160 and 250), so they're different variants. Within DN25 class 2, both rows ratio 400 — different Q3 options (10 vs 16 m³/h).\n\nGiven the instruction \"Lists of values ... is a MODEL MATRIX — emit model_level entries\", and the R137 example emits models per size, I think a reasonable approach:\n\n- models: one per unique (size, class, ratio, q3) combination? That's verbose but precise. Alternatively per size only, with model_level for per-size attributes (indicating range, verification scale interval) and matrix_tables preserving the full flow table.\n\nI'll go with: models per size+variant where variant = Q3 value (and class where it differs). Actually, let me define model_id as e.g. \"DN25/Q3=10/class 2\" — hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. The schema example for R137 uses model_id per size. The characteristics matrix varies per model. Here the natural primary model dimension is meter size; secondary dimension is accuracy class/flow configuration. The schema provides config_level for exactly this: \"attribute × config-axis matrix\" with values [{condition, value}].\n\nBut the flow attributes vary by BOTH size and class. The schema doesn't support two-dimensional matrices in model_level directly... except matrix_tables, which is meant to \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\".\n\nSo my plan:\n1. `matrix_tables[]`: \n   - Table 1: \"Flow characteristics by meter size and accuracy class\" with columns [meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_min, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1], rows = 36 rows (each data row).\n   - Table 2: \"Indicating range and verification scale interval by meter size\", rows = 5.\n2. `model_family.models`: one per meter size — 18 models: DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600. That's 16 sizes. Let me recount: DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 = 16 sizes.\n3. `characteristics.model_level`: per-size attributes:\n   - verification_scale_interval: varies by size group: DN25 → 0.00001; DN40–DN80 → 0.0001; DN100–DN250 → 0.001; DN300–DN450 → 0.01; DN500, DN600 → 0.1. Unit m³.\n   - indicating_range: 99999999 m³ for all sizes.\n   - maximum_admissible_pressure: DN200 and smaller → 16 bar(g); DN250 and larger → 10 bar(g). This varies by size, so model_level: values per model, or config_level with axis \"meter_size_group\". Since it's per-size, model_level works: 16 values.\n4. `characteristics.type_level`:\n   - min_max_admissible_temperature: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. (\"0,1/50\" → 0.1/50)\n   - environmental_class: \"C\"\n   - power_supply: battery... \"Battery 3,6 V, Type U_battery\". Value 3.6, unit V. Attribute: battery_voltage? The label is \"Power supply: Battery 3,6 V / Type U_battery\". Hmm, \"Type U_battery\" probably means \"U_battery\" is the symbol. I'll record: power_supply_type: \"Battery\"; power_supply_voltage: 3.6 V.\n   - orientation: \"All positions\"\n   - software_versions: [\"4.0.4\", \"4.0.10\", \"4.0.11\", \"4.0.12\", \"4.2.2\", \"4.2.4\", \"4.2.5\", \"4.2.6\"] — the trailing underscores in \"4.0.4_\" might be a formatting artifact (maybe italic markers in original). I'll list versions without underscores. Hmm, but \"don't fabricate\" — the underscore could be intentional? \"Software versions: 4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; ...\" — unlikely intentional; in original cert it's probably italic/other formatting. I'll strip them. Actually, to be safe, I could keep them as extracted... The instruction says don't fabricate, but stripping an obvious OCR/formatting artifact is reasonable. The original certificate (KROHNE WATERFLUX 3070, NMi R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 rev 4) lists software versions like \"4.0.4, 4.0.10...\". I'll strip underscores.\n   - measurement principle: electromagnetic (from description) — could add type_level: measurement_principle: \"electromagnetic\". Also \"designed to measure forward and reverse flow\", \"with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet\", \"no flow conditioner\", \"electronic calculating/indicating device\". These could be type_level string characteristics. I'll add a few key ones:\n     - measurement_principle: \"electromagnetic\"\n     - flow_directions: \"forward and reverse\"\n     - minimum_straight_inlet_outlet: \"0 D\" — value... hmm \"0 D\" (0 diameters). Could record as value 0, unit \"D\". Maybe skip or record as string. I'll record inlet_straight_length_min: 0, unit \"D\"? The rule says pure units are not values, but \"0 D\" has a value 0. I'll include straight_inlet_outlet_minimum: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"D\"}. Hmm, risky fabrication? It's clearly stated \"with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet\". I'll include it.\n     - flow_conditioner: \"none\" — value \"no flow conditioner\". Maybe as flow_conditioner: {value: \"none\"}. I'll include.\n   - accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"] at recommendation level.\n\n5. `recommendation`:\n   - The certificate attests conformity with R49-1/2006, R49-2/2006, R49-3/2006. The recommendation field is singular. The cert number references R49-1. I'll use id \"R49-1\" (canonical), edition 2006, scheme \"A\". Accuracy classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n   \n   Hmm, scheme A: OIML Certificates of Conformity for R49 issued under the old system are \"scheme A\" in the OIML-CS terminology. Yes.\n\n6. `test_reports[]`:\n   - R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, 41 pages, 14 annexes\n   - R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01, 40 pages, 3 annexes\n   - R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01, 40 pages, 4 annexes\n   - R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01, 40 pages, 3 annexes\n   - NMi-13200159-01, 6 pages, 1 annex\n   Role: type_evaluation_report.\n\n   The pages field: \"41 pages and 14 annexes\". I'll set pages: 41 and maybe add annexes in... schema only has id, date, pages, role. I'll set pages: 41. Dates: not given. Omit.\n\n7. `revision_history[]`:\n   - Initial, 28 March 2012, \"-\"\n   - 1, 22 October 2012, \"New software version added\"\n   - 2, 16 April 2013, \"New software version added\"\n   - 3, 26 September 2014, \"New software version added\"\n   - 4, 17 March 2015, \"Added sizes\"\n   \n   Note: certificate says issued 18 March 2015 but revision 4 dated 17 March 2015. Keep as-is.\n\n   Revision as string: \"Initial\", \"1\", \"2\", \"3\", \"4\".\n\n8. Footnotes: none in the markdown. Omit.\n\nNow let me decide on model granularity more concretely. I'll create models per (size) and encode the class/ratio variants in config_level with axis \"accuracy_class_and_ratio\" — no wait.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe cleaner to define models per unique size+class+ratio combination, using model_id like \"DN65 class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\". Then model_level attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio per model, plus verification_scale_interval and max pressure per model. That's very verbose (36+ models) but unambiguous.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: 16 size models + matrix table + config_level for class-varying attributes... but config_level values are {condition, value} without a model dimension, so q1 can't vary by both size and class there.\n\nGiven the schema's emphasis, I think the best is:\n- models per size (16).\n- matrix_tables with the full flow table (36 rows).\n- model_level: q1_min, q2, q3, q4 — but these have multiple values per size...\n\nHmm. OK here's another thought: within a size, the class-2 rows come first with ratio 400 (and DN80 class 2 has rows ratio 400 both: row1 100/0.25=400 ✓, row2 160/0.4=400 ✓). Wait DN80 class 2: row 1: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125 → ratio 400. Row 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200 → 160/0.4 = 400 ✓. So class 2 always ratio 400. Class 1 rows have ratio 160 or 250 (or 100 for DN600 which is \"1 or 2\" single row).\n\nFor sizes DN350–DN600, \"1 or 2\" class with single row and ratio 160 (100 for DN600). So these are dual-class meters with one flow set.\n\nSo variants within a size differ by (class, ratio) which determines Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4. Distinct variants per size:\n- DN25: (2,400,Q3=10), (2,400,Q3=16) → 2\n- DN40: (2,400,Q3=25), (2,400,Q3=40) → 2\n- DN50: (2,400,Q3=40), (2,400,Q3=63) → 2\n- DN65: (2,400,Q3=63), (2,400,Q3=100), (1,250,Q3=100) → 3\n- DN80: (2,400,Q3=100), (2,400,Q3=160), (1,160,Q3=100), (1,250,Q3=160) → 4\n- DN100: (2,400,Q3=160), (2,400,Q3=250), (1,160,Q3=160), (1,250,Q3=250) → 4\n- DN125: (2,400,Q3=250), (2,400,Q3=400), (1,160,Q3=250), (1,250,Q3=400) → 4\n- DN150: (2,400,Q3=400), (2,400,Q3=630), (1,160,Q3=400), (1,250,Q3=630) → 4\n- DN200: (2,400,Q3=630), (1,160,Q3=630) → 2\n- DN250: (2,400,Q3=1000), (1,160,Q3=1000) → 2\n- DN300: (2,400,Q3=1600), (1,160,Q3=1600) → 2\n- DN350: (1or2,160,Q3=2500) → 1\n- DN400: (1or2,160,Q3=4000) → 1\n- DN450: (1or2,160,Q3=4000) → 1\n- DN500: (1or2,160,Q3=6300) → 1\n- DN600: (1or2,100,Q3=6300) → 1\n\nTotal: 2+2+2+3+4+4+4+4+2+2+2+1+1+1+1+1 = 36 variants. Matches 36 rows.\n\n36 models is a lot but the schema explicitly wants model-level extraction. I'll do it: model_id like \"DN25-Q3=10-class2\" or more readable \"DN25, Q3=10 m³/h, class 2\". Hmm. Let me use a compact convention: \"DN25/class2/Q3=10\". \n\nThen model_level entries:\n- q1_minimum: values per model (36 values)\n- q2_transitional: 36 values\n- q3_permanent: 36 values\n- q4_overload: 36 values\n- ratio_q3_q1: 36 values\n- accuracy_class: 36 values (class \"2\", \"1\", \"1 or 2\")\n- verification_scale_interval: 36 values (per size group) — or keep per-size model_level. With 36 models, each model needs its VSI. Fine.\n- indicating_range: 99999999 for all — could be type_level since it's constant across all. Page 2 says \"Indicating range (m³): 99.999.999\" as a general characteristic. So type_level: indicating_range: 99999999 m³. And the page-4 table just confirms it per size. I'll put indicating_range at type_level and include the page-4 table in matrix_tables.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: varies by size (DN≤200: 16 bar; DN≥250: 10 bar). Put in model_level (16-size granularity) or config_level axis \"meter_size\". Since models are size+class variants, per-model values: 16 or 10 depending on size. I could do model_level with 36 values. Or config_level: axis \"meter_size_group\", values: [{condition: \"DN200 and smaller\", value: 16}, {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value: 10}]. That's cleaner and matches the cert wording. I'll use config_level for that.\n\nHmm, but wait — for model_level attributes with 36 models, this JSON gets huge. Is that OK? The schema says models[] one entry per variant, and model_level matrix. I think it's acceptable and most faithful. But maybe too heavy? Let me weigh: an alternative is models per size only (16), with matrix_tables preserving full flow table, and model_level for verification_scale_interval and max pressure. The q1..q4, ratio, class would live in matrix_tables only.\n\nThe rules emphasize: \"Detect this and put varying attributes in model_level, NOT type_level.\" The R137 example put qmax/qt/qmin in model_level per size model. Following that pattern: models per size, model_level for q-values... but q-values differ within a size across class/ratio variants. In R137, each size had exactly one row. Here sizes have up to 4 rows.\n\nI think the most correct representation: models = 36 variants (size × class × ratio / Q3). Then model_level q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 each with 36 values. That fully captures the table without loss. Plus matrix_tables for the two tables.\n\nActually, that duplicates data (matrix_tables + model_level), which the R137 example explicitly does too (\"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\"). So duplication is intended by the schema.\n\nLet me just be careful and generate the 36-model version. Actually, hold on — that's going to be a very long JSON. Let me estimate: 36 models × 7 model_level attributes × ~4 lines each ≈ manageable but long. I'll do it. Actually, let me reconsider whether to reduce.\n\nAlternative middle ground: models per size (16 models, model_id \"DN25\" etc.), and model_level for attributes that are single-valued per size... but q1 etc. aren't. So the q-attributes would only be in matrix_tables. That loses model_level for the primary metrological attributes, which seems against the spirit.\n\nI'll go with 36 variants. Model naming: I'll use the format \"DN80/class1/Q3=100m3h\"... hmm readability. Let me use: model_id: \"DN25-Q3=10-class 2\". Or maybe \"DN25 | class 2 | Q3=10\". JSON-safe simple: \"DN25_Q3=10_class2\". I'll use hyphens: \"DN25-Q3=10-class2\".\n\nWait, but for DN80 class 1 there are two variants with Q3=100 (ratio 160) and Q3=160 (ratio 250). Q3 disambiguates. For DN25 class 2 both ratio 400, Q3 10 vs 16 disambiguates. Good — model_id = size + Q3 + class is unique across all 36. Let me verify DN100: class2 Q3=160 (ratio 400), class2 Q3=250 (ratio 400), class1 Q3=160 (ratio 160), class1 Q3=250 (ratio 250). Unique ✓. DN350: class \"1 or 2\", Q3=2500. ✓.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: DN65 class 1 row: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, ratio 250. And DN65 class 2 row 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, ratio 400. Different Q1, same Q3=100. Model ids: \"DN65-Q3=100-class2\" vs \"DN65-Q3=100-class1\" — unique ✓.\n\nNow define attributes per model:\n- accuracy_class: \"1\", \"2\", or \"1 or 2\" (string values)\n- q1_minimum (m³/h)\n- q2_transitional (m³/h)\n- q3_permanent (m³/h)\n- q4_overload (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n- verification_scale_interval (m³) — per size group\n- indicating_range — constant 99999999, put at type_level only.\n- maximum_pressure — config_level with axis meter_size group... but with per-model values being size-dependent, config_level fits: conditions \"DN200 and smaller\" → 16 bar(g), \"DN250 and larger\" → 10 bar(g). I'll do config_level.\n\nActually wait — should accuracy_class be a model_level attribute? Sure, it varies by model.\n\nValue objects need the strict shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. For each model_level entry, the shape is:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"q1_minimum\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"unit_id\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"DN25-Q3=10-class2\", \"value\": 0.025}, ...]}\n```\nHmm, the schema shows values entries as {\"model\", \"value\", \"footnote_markers[]\"}. In the R137 example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]}. So unit_symbol at the attribute level, value plain. I'll follow that: unit_symbol on the attribute, plain values per model. Should I include unit_id at attribute level? The strict rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with value/unit... but the examples in rule 4 show flat numbers with unit_symbol at attribute level. The examples take precedence for matrix entries. I'll follow the example format exactly (attribute, unit_symbol, values[{model, value}]) and optionally add unit_id: null. The example doesn't include unit_id; I'll add it for consistency with rule 3? The R137 example omits unit_id. I'll omit unit_id in model_level to match the example, or include null... I'll include \"unit_id\": null — harmless. Hmm, actually to match examples precisely I'll omit it. Let me include it as null for the caller. The R137 example output omits it. I'll match the example: omit.\n\nFor type_level: {label: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}} — per rule 2 strict shape.\n\nNow, config_level values: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. E.g.:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"maximum_admissible_pressure\", \"axis\": \"meter_size_group\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"DN200 and smaller\", \"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}, {\"condition\": \"DN250 and larger\", \"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}]}\n```\n\nNow decimal conversion: all the table commas → dots: 0,025 → 0.025; 12,5 → 12.5; 31,3 → 31.3; 312,5 → 312.5; 78,75 → 78.75; 1,02 → 1.02; etc.\n\nLet me now write out all 36 model rows with converted numbers:\n\n1. DN25, class 2, Q1 0.025, Q2 0.04, Q3 10, Q4 12.5, ratio 400 → model \"DN25-Q3=10-class2\"\n2. DN25, class 2, Q1 0.04, Q2 0.064, Q3 16, Q4 20, ratio 400 → \"DN25-Q3=16-class2\"\n3. DN40, class 2, Q1 0.0625, Q2 0.1, Q3 25, Q4 31.3, ratio 400 → \"DN40-Q3=25-class2\"\n4. DN40, class 2, Q1 0.1, Q2 0.16, Q3 40, Q4 50, ratio 400 → \"DN40-Q3=40-class2\"\n5. DN50, class 2, Q1 0.1, Q2 0.16, Q3 40, Q4 50, ratio 400 → \"DN50-Q3=40-class2\"\n6. DN50, class 2, Q1 0.1575, Q2 0.252, Q3 63, Q4 78.75, ratio 400 → \"DN50-Q3=63-class2\"\n7. DN65, class 2, Q1 0.1575, Q2 0.25, Q3 63, Q4 78.75, ratio 400 → \"DN65-Q3=63-class2\"\n8. DN65, class 2, Q1 0.25, Q2 0.4, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 400 → \"DN65-Q3=100-class2\"\n9. DN65, class 1, Q1 0.4, Q2 0.64, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 250 → \"DN65-Q3=100-class1\"\n10. DN80, class 2, Q1 0.25, Q2 0.4, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 400 → \"DN80-Q3=100-class2\"\n11. DN80, class 2, Q1 0.4, Q2 0.64, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 400 → \"DN80-Q3=160-class2\"\n12. DN80, class 1, Q1 0.625, Q2 1, Q3 100, Q4 125, ratio 160 → \"DN80-Q3=100-class1\"\n13. DN80, class 1, Q1 0.64, Q2 1.02, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 250 → \"DN80-Q3=160-class1\"\n14. DN100, class 2, Q1 0.4, Q2 0.64, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 400 → \"DN100-Q3=160-class2\"\n15. DN100, class 2, Q1 0.625, Q2 1, Q3 250, Q4 312.5, ratio 400 → \"DN100-Q3=250-class2\"\n16. DN100, class 1, Q1 1, Q2 1.6, Q3 160, Q4 200, ratio 160 → \"DN100-Q3=160-class1\"\n17. DN100, class 1, Q1 1, Q2 1.6, Q3 250, Q4 312.5, ratio 250 → \"DN100-Q3=250-class1\"\n18. DN125, class 2, Q1 0.625, Q2 1, Q3 250, Q4 312.5, ratio 400 → \"DN125-Q3=250-class2\"\n19. DN125, class 2, Q1 1, Q2 1.6, Q3 400, Q4 500, ratio 400 → \"DN125-Q3=400-class2\"\n20. DN125, class 1, Q1 1.56, Q2 2.5, Q3 250, Q4 312.5, ratio 160 → \"DN125-Q3=250-class1\"\n21. DN125, class 1, Q1 1.6, Q2 2.56, Q3 400, Q4 500, ratio 250 → \"DN125-Q3=400-class1\"\n22. DN150, class 2, Q1 1, Q2 1.6, Q3 400, Q4 500, ratio 400 → \"DN150-Q3=400-class2\"\n23. DN150, class 2, Q1 1.575, Q2 2.52, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 400 → \"DN150-Q3=630-class2\"\n24. DN150, class 1, Q1 2.5, Q2 4, Q3 400, Q4 500, ratio 160 → \"DN150-Q3=400-class1\"\n25. DN150, class 1, Q1 2.52, Q2 4.03, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 250 → \"DN150-Q3=630-class1\"\n26. DN200, class 2, Q1 1.575, Q2 2.52, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 400 → \"DN200-Q3=630-class2\"\n27. DN200, class 1, Q1 3.94, Q2 6.3, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 160 → \"DN200-Q3=630-class1\"\n28. DN250, class 2, Q1 2.5, Q2 4, Q3 1000, Q4 1250, ratio 400 → \"DN250-Q3=1000-class2\"\n29. DN250, class 1, Q1 6.25, Q2 10, Q3 1000, Q4 1250, ratio 160 → \"DN250-Q3=1000-class1\"\n30. DN300, class 2, Q1 4, Q2 6.4, Q3 1600, Q4 2000, ratio 400 → \"DN300-Q3=1600-class2\"\n31. DN300, class 1, Q1 10, Q2 16, Q3 1600, Q4 2000, ratio 160 → \"DN300-Q3=1600-class1\"\n32. DN350, class 1 or 2, Q1 15.625, Q2 25, Q3 2500, Q4 3125, ratio 160 → \"DN350-Q3=2500-class1or2\"\n33. DN400, class 1 or 2, Q1 25, Q2 40, Q3 4000, Q4 5000, ratio 160 → \"DN400-Q3=4000-class1or2\"\n34. DN450, class 1 or 2, Q1 25, Q2 40, Q3 4000, Q4 5000, ratio 160 → \"DN450-Q3=4000-class1or2\"\n35. DN500, class 1 or 2, Q1 39.375, Q2 63, Q3 6300, Q4 7875, ratio 160 → \"DN500-Q3=6300-class1or2\"\n36. DN600, class 1 or 2, Q1 63, Q2 100.8, Q3 6300, Q4 7875, ratio 100 → \"DN600-Q3=6300-class1or2\"\n\nVerification scale interval per model (from table 2):\n- DN25: 0.00001 m³\n- DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80: 0.0001 m³\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250: 0.001 m³\n- DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 0.01 m³\n- DN500, DN600: 0.1 m³\n\nThat's a lot of model_level entries. Verification scale interval: 36 values. Hmm, this makes the JSON enormous. \n\nAlternative: Keep verification_scale_interval as model_level but at size granularity? The schema's model_level values reference \"model\" — must match model_id. So if models are 36, VSI values need model refs from the 36.\n\nI could reduce total size by making VSI a config_level attribute with axis \"meter_size_group\":\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval\", \"axis\": \"meter_size\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"DN25\", \"value\": 0.00001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65 and DN80\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200 and DN250\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"DN300, DN350, DN400 and DN450\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"DN500 and DN600\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n]}\n```\nThat's clean and matches the source table's grouping. Similarly max pressure config_level. Good.\n\nAnd keep model_level for the q-values, ratio, accuracy_class (36 each). Accuracy class as string \"1\", \"2\", \"1 or 2\".\n\nAlso indicating_range: constant across all — type_level: {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}. Note \"99.999.999\" — European thousands separator → 99999999.\n\nmatrix_tables: I'll include both tables:\n1. \"Flow rates by meter size and accuracy class\": columns [meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1], column_units [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null], rows: 36 entries. Should I flatten rowspan'd cells (repeating DN25/class 2 on each row)? Yes — rows as flat objects is cleaner. For rowspan'd ratio (e.g., 400 for both DN25 rows), repeat 400.\n\nLet me write rows:\n1. {meter_size: \"DN25\", accuracy_class: \"2\", q1_minimum: 0.025, q2_transitional: 0.04, q3_permanent: 10, q4_overload: 12.5, ratio_q3_q1: 400}\n2. {DN25, 2, 0.04, 0.064, 16, 20, 400}\n3. {DN40, 2, 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.3, 400}\n4. {DN40, 2, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400}\n5. {DN50, 2, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400}\n6. {DN50, 2, 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400}\n7. {DN65, 2, 0.1575, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400}\n8. {DN65, 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400}\n9. {DN65, 1, 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250}\n10. {DN80, 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400}\n11. {DN80, 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400}\n12. {DN80, 1, 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160}\n13. {DN80, 1, 0.64, 1.02, 160, 200, 250}\n14. {DN100, 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400}\n15. {DN100, 2, 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400}\n16. {DN100, 1, 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160}\n17. {DN100, 1, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250}\n18. {DN125, 2, 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400}\n19. {DN125, 2, 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400}\n20. {DN125, 1, 1.56, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160}\n21. {DN125, 1, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250}\n22. {DN150, 2, 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400}\n23. {DN150, 2, 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400}\n24. {DN150, 1, 2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160}\n25. {DN150, 1, 2.52, 4.03, 630, 787.5, 250}\n26. {DN200, 2, 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400}\n27. {DN200, 1, 3.94, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160}\n28. {DN250, 2, 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400}\n29. {DN250, 1, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160}\n30. {DN300, 2, 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400}\n31. {DN300, 1, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160}\n32. {DN350, \"1 or 2\", 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160}\n33. {DN400, \"1 or 2\", 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160}\n34. {DN450, \"1 or 2\", 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160}\n35. {DN500, \"1 or 2\", 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160}\n36. {DN600, \"1 or 2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100}\n\n2. \"Indicating range and verification scale interval by meter size\": columns [meter_size, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval], column_units [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"], rows:\n- {meter_size: \"DN25\", indicating_range: 99999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.00001}\n- {meter_size: \"DN40, DN50, DN65 and DN80\", indicating_range: 99999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.0001}\n- {meter_size: \"DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200 and DN250\", indicating_range: 99999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.001}\n- {meter_size: \"DN300, DN350, DN400 and DN450\", indicating_range: 99999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.01}\n- {meter_size: \"DN500 and DN600\", indicating_range: 99999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.1}\n\nNow the schema for matrix_tables in the example includes \"column_units\" — shown in the example even though the yaml schema only lists columns, rows, description, footnotes. The example includes column_units, so I'll include it.\n\nNow model_level with 36 values each × 6 attributes (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio, accuracy_class). That's a lot of JSON but let's do it. Actually, should accuracy_class be model_level? Yes, it varies per model.\n\nLet me think about attribute naming per rule 1:\n- \"Min. Q1\" → q1_minimum. Hmm, Q1 is minimum flow rate. Canonical: \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\"? Rule 1 examples: \"Max. number of load cell intervals nLC\" → max_load_cell_intervals_nlc. So \"Min. Q1\" → \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\" or \"q1_minimum\". I'll use \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\"? Hmm. Table header: \"Min. Q1\", \"Trans. Q2\", \"Perm. Q3\", \"Over. Q4\". I'll use:\n  - \"q1_minimum\" — hmm, maybe \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\" is more descriptive. Let me use \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", \"transitional_flow_rate_q2\", \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\". These are clear.\n\nFor consistency, model_id could be like \"DN25/Q3=10/class2\". I'll use \"DN25-Q3=10-class2\" style. Slightly ugly with class \"1 or 2\" → \"class1or2\". Alternatively \"DN350-Q3=2500\". Since \"1 or 2\" means it satisfies both classes at that flow. For model ids: \"DN350-Q3=2500-class1or2\" is odd. Maybe just append class when single: for \"1 or 2\" use \"class1-2\"? I'll use \"DN350-Q3=2500-class1or2\". Fine.\n\nHmm, actually maybe cleaner: use model_id like \"DN25/Q3=10\" and put class as attribute only. Is size+Q3 unique? Check: DN65 Q3=100 appears twice: class2 (ratio 400) and class1 (ratio 250). DN80 Q3=100 twice (class2 ratio400, class1 ratio160). DN80 Q3=160: class2 (ratio 400) and class1 (ratio 250). DN100 Q3=160: class2 & class1. DN100 Q3=250: class2 & class1. DN125 Q3=250: class2 & class1. DN125 Q3=400: class2 & class1. DN150 Q3=400: class2&1. DN150 Q3=630: class2&1. DN200 Q3=630: class2&1. DN250 Q3=1000: 2&1. DN300 Q3=1600: 2&1. So size+Q3 is NOT unique. Must include class. OK: \"DN65-Q3=100-class2\" vs \"DN65-Q3=100-class1\".\n\nFor \"1 or 2\" single rows, model_id \"DN350-Q3=2500-class1or2\". OK.\n\nNow type_level characteristics:\n- min_max_admissible_temperature: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}. Label: \"min_max_admissible_temperature\".\n- environmental_class: {value: \"C\", unit_symbol: null, ...}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"Battery\"} — hmm the cert says \"Power supply: Battery 3,6 V / Type U_battery\". Interpretation: battery-powered, 3.6 V. \"Type U_battery\" — probably the type designation is \"U_battery\"? Or the battery voltage symbol. I'll record:\n  - power_supply_type: \"battery\"\n  - battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - Maybe battery_type: \"U_battery\"? That seems like the cell type designation. I'll add power_supply: \"Battery 3.6 V, type U_battery\"? Hmm. I'll do battery_voltage 3.6 V and battery_type \"U_battery\". Actually \"Type U_battery\" likely refers to battery type designation. I'll include it as battery_type: \"U_battery\".\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions\"}\n- indicating_range: {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- measurement_principle: \"electromagnetic\"\n- flow_directions: \"forward and reverse\" — or bidirectional. I'll record as string \"forward and reverse\".\n- straight_inlet_outlet: \"minimum 0 D\" — I'll record straight_pipe_length_inlet_outlet_minimum: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"D\"}. Hmm, \"D\" = nominal diameter multiples. OK.\n- flow_conditioner: {value: \"none\"} — the description says \"with no flow conditioner\". Record flow_conditioner: \"none\".\n- device_type: \"electronic calculating/indicating device\" — could be module_designation: \"Electronic calculating/indicating device\". The schema's module_designation examples are for R60 analog load cell etc. For a water meter, I'll set module_designation: \"Electronic calculating/indicating device\" — reasonable, it's the indicating device module. Or null. The cert says \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\". I'll put that as module_designation.\n- software_versions: {value: [\"4.0.4\",\"4.0.10\",\"4.0.11\",\"4.0.12\",\"4.2.2\",\"4.2.4\",\"4.2.5\",\"4.2.6\"]} — array as value. Rule 2 says value is number|{min,max}|string. A list isn't in the allowed shapes... but software versions are legitimately a list. Hmm. The schema for values allows string. I could make value a single string \"4.0.4, 4.0.10, 4.0.11, 4.0.12, 4.2.2, 4.2.4, 4.2.5, 4.2.6\". That preserves data. Or array — json.loads handles arrays fine; the rule about strict shape says value: <number | {min,max} | string>. To comply, I'll use the comma-joined string. Hmm, but that loses structure. Rule 2's \"Lists of values\" clause: \"25,30,50,60,75,90 (multiple Emax values) is a MODEL MATRIX — emit model_level entries, NOT a list-as-value.\" By analogy, software versions... they're not per-model attributes though. Software versions are alternatives for the whole type. A model matrix over software versions? Could do model_level with 8 models (\"software 4.0.4\" etc.)? That conflicts with the size-based models. No — I'll keep it as a list-valued... Let me just use a string value with comma separation: value: \"4.0.4, 4.0.10, 4.0.11, 4.0.12, 4.2.2, 4.2.4, 4.2.5, 4.2.6\". Safe and parseable. Actually arrays are more useful... but rule compliance: string. I'll use an array? Hmm.\n\nThe schema says value: <number | {min, max} | string>. I'll go with the array since JSON arrays are natural and the caller likely tolerates; no — \"STRICT\" is emphasized. String it is. Actually wait, maybe I can do both: type_level \"software_versions\": {\"value\": [\"4.0.4\", ...]}. Risky vs. instruction. I'll use string.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: the revision history says revisions 1-3 added software versions, revision 4 added sizes. The versions listed are the current allowed set.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49-1\" — hmm, canonical. The certificate covers R49-1, R49-2, R49-3. The certificate number is \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\". The recommendation field is singular. I'll use id: \"R49-1\", edition: 2006, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"]. Should I mention R49-2/2006 and R49-3/2006? The schema has one recommendation object. The primary is R49-1 (Metrological and technical requirements) — the cert of conformity for R49 typically references the certificate number R49-1/2006. I'll add the others... there's no field. Maybe keep just R49-1. Hmm, but the certificate attests conformity to all three parts. The schema is singular; I'll pick R49-1 and note edition 2006. Accuracy classes: R49 classes are \"1 and 2\" — from description \"class 1 and 2\". Accuracy_classes: [\"1\",\"2\"].\n\nCertificate number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\", revision 4. Should the number include revision? The field is `number` — I'll put \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\" and there's no revision field at cert level; revision_history captures revisions. The header says \"Number R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 revision 4\". I'll set number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\". Maybe include revision in number? No, keep clean.\n\nScheme \"A\": OIML-CS scheme A = certificate of conformity. Yes.\n\nproject_number: \"13200159\". page_total: 4. member_state: \"The Netherlands\". date_issued: \"2015-03-18\" or \"18 March 2015\"? Format not specified; ISO is safer for parsing: \"2015-03-18\". I'll use ISO dates throughout. Revision history dates: 2012-03-28, 2012-10-22, 2013-04-16, 2014-09-26, 2015-03-17.\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"C. Oosterman\"\n- person_title: \"Head Certification Board\"\n- phone: \"+31 78 6332332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"KROHNE Altometer\", address_lines: [\"Kerkeplaat 12\", \"3313 LC Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same (cert says \"Applicant and Manufacturer\"). Include identical entry.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- module_designation: \"Electronic calculating/indicating device\" — hmm, or null. The R60 example: \"Analog load cell\". For this cert, there's no explicit module designation section. The description mentions electronic calculating/indicating device. I'll set it — seems in spirit.\n- description: combine: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, model WATERFLUX 3070, class 1 and 2, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\"\n\ntest_reports: role \"type_evaluation_report\" for all five. dates unknown → omit date. pages: 41, 40, 40, 40, 6. Annexes: maybe include in... schema has no annex field. Could add \"annexes\": 14 — extra field beyond schema; rule 8 says omit what's not present; adding extra fields is mild. The schema fields: id, date, pages, role. I'll add annexes as an extra key? Schema adherence — I'll add it since it's part of the data; extra keys generally tolerated. Hmm, \"Don't fabricate\" refers to making things up. Adding an \"annexes\" field is honest. I'll include it. Actually, to be safe I'll include annexes — it preserves info. Yes.\n\nComponents: The cert doesn't name sub-assemblies separately (just \"electronic calculating/indicating device\" integrated). I'll omit components.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nConfig-level:\n```json\n[\n  {\"attribute\": \"maximum_admissible_pressure\", \"axis\": \"meter_size\", \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN200 and smaller\", \"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN250 and larger\", \"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}\n  ]},\n  {\"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval\", \"axis\": \"meter_size\", \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN25\", \"value\": 0.00001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65 and DN80\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200 and DN250\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN300, DN350, DN400 and DN450\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN500 and DN600\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n  ]}\n]\n```\n\nHmm wait — but the q-attributes at model_level and VSI at config_level: is that inconsistent? VSI is genuinely size-dependent, and models include size. Could put VSI in model_level (36 values). I chose config_level to mirror the certificate's grouping. Both fine. I'll keep config_level for both pressure and VSI. Actually, let me reconsider: config_level is described as \"attribute × config-axis matrix\" for configurations (e.g., static vs dynamic). Meter size group is a config axis of sorts. OK.\n\nNow build model_level entries. Values:\n\naccuracy_class (string values):\nDN25-Q3=10-class2: \"2\"\nDN25-Q3=16-class2: \"2\"\nDN40-Q3=25-class2: \"2\"\nDN40-Q3=40-class2: \"2\"\nDN50-Q3=40-class2: \"2\"\nDN50-Q3=63-class2: \"2\"\nDN65-Q3=63-class2: \"2\"\nDN65-Q3=100-class2: \"2\"\nDN65-Q3=100-class1: \"1\"\nDN80-Q3=100-class2: \"2\"\nDN80-Q3=160-class2: \"2\"\nDN80-Q3=100-class1: \"1\"\nDN80-Q3=160-class1: \"1\"\nDN100-Q3=160-class2: \"2\"\nDN100-Q3=250-class2: \"2\"\nDN100-Q3=160-class1: \"1\"\nDN100-Q3=250-class1: \"1\"\nDN125-Q3=250-class2: \"2\"\nDN125-Q3=400-class2: \"2\"\nDN125-Q3=250-class1: \"1\"\nDN125-Q3=400-class1: \"1\"\nDN150-Q3=400-class2: \"2\"\nDN150-Q3=630-class2: \"2\"\nDN150-Q3=400-class1: \"1\"\nDN150-Q3=630-class1: \"1\"\nDN200-Q3=630-class2: \"2\"\nDN200-Q3=630-class1: \"1\"\nDN250-Q3=1000-class2: \"2\"\nDN250-Q3=1000-class1: \"1\"\nDN300-Q3=1600-class2: \"2\"\nDN300-Q3=1600-class1: \"1\"\nDN350-Q3=2500-class1or2: \"1 or 2\"\nDN400-Q3=4000-class1or2: \"1 or 2\"\nDN450-Q3=4000-class1or2: \"1 or 2\"\nDN500-Q3=6300-class1or2: \"1 or 2\"\nDN600-Q3=6300-class1or2: \"1 or 2\"\n\nThis is getting long but fine. Let me now also double check \"DN200 class 1\" Q1: markdown says 3,94 → 3.94. Yes (\"3,94\").\n\nAlso DN125 class1 row1 Q1 \"1,56\" → 1.56 (as printed).\n\nAlso DN40 class2 row1 Q4 \"31,3\" → 31.3. Note Q4 should be 1.25×Q3 = 31.25, printed as 31.3. Keep 31.3.\n\nDN50 row2 Q1 \"0,1575\" → 0.1575, Q2 \"0,252\" → 0.252, Q4 \"78,75\" → 78.75.\nDN65 row1: Q1 0.1575, Q2 \"0,25\" → 0.25, Q3 63, Q4 78.75.\nDN80 class1 row1: \"0,625\" → 0.625, Q2 \"1\" → 1.\nDN80 class1 row2: Q1 0.64, Q2 \"1,02\" → 1.02.\nDN100 class2 row2: Q1 \"0,625\" → 0.625, Q4 \"312,5\" → 312.5.\nDN125 class1 row1: Q1 \"1,56\" → 1.56, Q2 \"2,5\" → 2.5.\nDN125 class1 row2: Q2 \"2,56\" → 2.56.\nDN150 class2 row2: Q1 \"1,575\" → 1.575, Q2 \"2,52\" → 2.52, Q4 \"787,5\" → 787.5.\nDN150 class1 row1: Q1 \"2,5\" → 2.5.\nDN150 class1 row2: Q1 \"2,52\" → 2.52, Q2 \"4,03\" → 4.03.\nDN200 class2: Q1 \"1,575\" → 1.575, Q2 \"2,52\" → 2.52, Q4 \"787,5\" → 787.5.\nDN200 class1: Q1 \"3,94\" → 3.94, Q2 \"6,3\" → 6.3, Q4 \"787,5\" → 787.5.\nDN250 class2: Q1 \"2,5\" → 2.5.\nDN300 class2: Q2 \"6,4\" → 6.4.\nDN350: Q1 \"15,625\" → 15.625.\nDN400/450: Q1 25, Q2 40, Q3 4000, Q4 5000.\nDN500: Q1 \"39,375\" → 39.375, Q2 63, Q3 6300, Q4 \"7875\" → 7875.\nDN600: Q1 63, Q2 \"100,8\" → 100.8, Q3 6300, Q4 \"7875\" → 7875.\n\nMin temp \"0,1/50\" → 0.1/50: {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n\nBattery \"3,6 V\" → 3.6 V.\n\nOK. Software versions: \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_; 4.2.5_; 4.2.6_\". The trailing underscore likely OCR artifact of formatting. I'll strip: [\"4.0.4\", \"4.0.10\", \"4.0.11\", \"4.0.12\", \"4.2.2\", \"4.2.4\", \"4.2.5\", \"4.2.6\"]. As string: \"4.0.4, 4.0.10, 4.0.11, 4.0.12, 4.2.2, 4.2.4, 4.2.5, 4.2.6\". I'll use the string.\n\nHmm — actually, thinking again, maybe I should include software versions as an array under a non-characteristic place? No — type_level with string value is fine.\n\nUnit for indicating range: \"m³\". 99.999.999 → 99999999.\n\nNow model_family:\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"WATERFLUX 3070\",\n  \"models\": [ {\"model_id\": \"DN25-Q3=10-class2\"}, ... 36 entries ]\n}\n```\nfamily_name: \"WATERFLUX 3070\" — the family name is the type name. OK.\n\nmatrix_tables: two tables as described.\n\nAlso should model_level include \"meter_size\"? The size is embedded in model_id; adding a meter_size attribute would be redundant but explicit. I'll add \"meter_size\" as model_level attribute with values \"DN25\" etc.? That's 36 more entries. Hmm — helpful for consumers. I'll include it as the first model_level attribute. Value strings. Yes, I'll include — attribute \"meter_size\", unit null. Wait, unit_symbol null. Let me structure: {\"attribute\": \"meter_size\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": \"DN25\"}, ...]}. OK.\n\nOrder of model_level entries: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio.\n\nLet me also double-check accuracy_class values for DN350-600: \"1 or 2\" — meaning meter can be class 1 or 2 at those flows? The table says Accuracy class \"1 or 2\" with one flow row. So the meter meets both classes... anyway extract as-is.\n\nNote: description says \"class 1 and 2\" — consistent.\n\nRecommendation accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\nNow write the final JSON. Keep it valid: no trailing commas, proper escapes. Use \"m³\" character directly (JSON supports UTF-8). \"°C\" fine.\n\nType-level characteristics list:\n- minimum_admissible_temperature? It's min/max combined: \"min_max_admissible_temperature\": {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- indicating_range: {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\", unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"C\", ...}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"Battery\", ...}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\", ...}\n- battery_type: {value: \"U_battery\", ...} — hmm is that fabrication? The markdown literally: \"Power supply : Battery 3,6 V / Type / U_battery\". It reads like a small table: Power supply → Battery 3,6 V; Type → U_battery. So the battery type is \"U_battery\". Hmm, or \"U_battery\" denotes voltage symbol. Including battery_type: \"U_battery\" is literal extraction. I'll include it.\n- measurement_principle: {value: \"electromagnetic\", ...}\n- flow_directions: {value: \"forward and reverse\", ...}\n- minimum_straight_inlet_outlet: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"D\", ...} — label maybe \"straight_inlet_outlet_length_minimum\". I'll name \"straight_inlet_outlet_minimum_diameters\"? Keep simpler: \"straight_inlet_outlet\": value \"minimum 0 D\"? Rule 1 wants snake_case labels, values structured. I'll use label \"straight_inlet_outlet_length\" with value {min: 0, max: null}? \"with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet\" → minimum 0 D, so value: {min: 0, max: null}, unit_symbol: \"D\". I'll do that: attribute \"straight_inlet_outlet_length\".\n- flow_conditioner: {value: \"none\", ...}\n- software_versions: {value: \"4.0.4, 4.0.10, 4.0.11, 4.0.12, 4.2.2, 4.2.4, 4.2.5, 4.2.6\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- accuracy_classes... that's in recommendation.\n- indication_device: covered by module_designation.\n\nShould footnote_markers/unit_id be included with null? Rule 2 shape includes them. I'll include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] where applicable. Keep consistent.\n\nNow matrix table 1 name: \"Flow rates by meter size and accuracy class\". columns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"], column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]. description: \"Permissible flow rates for each meter size and accuracy class; Q1–Q4 in m³/h.\" footnotes: [].\n\nTable 2 name: \"Indicating range and verification scale interval by meter size\", columns [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range\", \"verification_scale_interval\"], column_units [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"], rows as above with 99999999.\n\nmodel_level arrays — write them out.\n\nmeter_size values (36):\nDN25-Q3=10-class2 → DN25\nDN25-Q3=16-class2 → DN25\nDN40-Q3=25-class2 → DN40\nDN40-Q3=40-class2 → DN40\nDN50-Q3=40-class2 → DN50\nDN50-Q3=63-class2 → DN50\nDN65-Q3=63-class2 → DN65\nDN65-Q3=100-class2 → DN65\nDN65-Q3=100-class1 → DN65\nDN80-Q3=100-class2 → DN80\nDN80-Q3=160-class2 → DN80\nDN80-Q3=100-class1 → DN80\nDN80-Q3=160-class1 → DN80\nDN100-Q3=160-class2 → DN100\nDN100-Q3=250-class2 → DN100\nDN100-Q3=160-class1 → DN100\nDN100-Q3=250-class1 → DN100\nDN125-Q3=250-class2 → DN125\nDN125-Q3=400-class2 → DN125\nDN125-Q3=250-class1 → DN125\nDN125-Q3=400-class1 → DN125\nDN150-Q3=400-class2 → DN150\nDN150-Q3=630-class2 → DN150\nDN150-Q3=400-class1 → DN150\nDN150-Q3=630-class1 → DN150\nDN200-Q3=630-class2 → DN200\nDN200-Q3=630-class1 → DN200\nDN250-Q3=1000-class2 → DN250\nDN250-Q3=1000-class1 → DN250\nDN300-Q3=1600-class2 → DN300\nDN300-Q3=1600-class1 → DN300\nDN350-Q3=2500-class1or2 → DN350\nDN400-Q3=4000-class1or2 → DN400\nDN450-Q3=4000-class1or2 → DN450\nDN500-Q3=6300-class1or2 → DN500\nDN600-Q3=6300-class1or2 → DN600\n\nq1 (m³/h): 0.025, 0.04, 0.0625, 0.1, 0.1, 0.1575, 0.1575, 0.25, 0.4, 0.25, 0.4, 0.625, 0.64, 0.4, 0.625, 1, 1, 0.625, 1, 1.56, 1.6, 1, 1.575, 2.5, 2.52, 1.575, 3.94, 2.5, 6.25, 4, 10, 15.625, 25, 25, 39.375, 63\n\nq2: 0.04, 0.064, 0.1, 0.16, 0.16, 0.252, 0.25, 0.4, 0.64, 0.4, 0.64, 1, 1.02, 0.64, 1, 1.6, 1.6, 1, 1.6, 2.5, 2.56, 1.6, 2.52, 4, 4.03, 2.52, 6.3, 4, 10, 6.4, 16, 25, 40, 40, 63, 100.8\n\nq3: 10, 16, 25, 40, 40, 63, 63, 100, 100, 100, 160, 100, 160, 160, 250, 160, 250, 250, 400, 250, 400, 400, 630, 400, 630, 630, 630, 1000, 1000, 1600, 1600, 2500, 4000, 4000, 6300, 6300\n\nq4: 12.5, 20, 31.3, 50, 50, 78.75, 78.75, 125, 125, 125, 200, 125, 200, 200, 312.5, 200, 312.5, 312.5, 500, 312.5, 500, 500, 787.5, 500, 787.5, 787.5, 787.5, 1250, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 5000, 5000, 7875, 7875\n\nratio: 400, 400, 400, 400, 400, 400, 400, 400, 250, 400, 400, 160, 250, 400, 400, 160, 250, 400, 400, 160, 250, 400, 400, 160, 250, 400, 160, 400, 160, 400, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 100\n\naccuracy_class: 2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,2,1,2,1,\"1 or 2\",\"1 or 2\",\"1 or 2\",\"1 or 2\",\"1 or 2\"\n\nCross-check ratio per model:\n- idx9 (DN65-Q3=100-class1): 100/0.4 = 250 ✓\n- idx12 (DN80-Q3=100-class1): 100/0.625 = 160 ✓\n- idx13 (DN80-Q3=160-class1): 160/0.64 = 250 ✓\n- idx16 (DN100-Q3=160-class1): 160/1 = 160 ✓\n- idx17: 250/1 = 250 ✓\n- idx20 (DN125-Q3=250-class1): 250/1.56 = 160.26 — cert printed 1.56 with ratio 160; keep as printed ✓\n- idx21: 400/1.6 = 250 ✓\n- idx24 (DN150-Q3=630-class1): 630/2.52 = 250 ✓\n- idx27 (DN200-Q3=630-class1): 630/3.94 = 159.9 ≈ 160 ✓ as printed\n- idx29 (DN250-Q3=1000-class1): 1000/6.25 = 160 ✓\n- idx31 (DN300-Q3=1600-class1): 1600/10 = 160 ✓\n- idx32 (DN350): 2500/15.625 = 160 ✓\n- idx36 (DN600): 6300/63 = 100 ✓\n\nGood.\n\nNow the JSON size: it's long but necessary. Let me also decide on attribute names:\n- \"meter_size\" (string, unit null)\n- \"accuracy_class\" (string)\n- \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", unit \"m³/h\"\n- \"transitional_flow_rate_q2\", unit \"m³/h\"\n- \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", unit \"m³/h\"\n- \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", unit \"m³/h\"\n- \"ratio_q3_q1\", unit null\n\nNow certificate-level fields:\n\n```json\n\"certificate\": {\n  \"number\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"13200159\",\n  \"page_total\": 4,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2015-03-18\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\nShould I include revision in number? \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 revision 4\". There's no cert-level revision field. revision_history covers it. Keep number without revision.\n\nrecommendation:\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"R49-1\",\n  \"edition\": 2006,\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"accuracy_classes\": [\"1\", \"2\"]\n}\n```\nHmm — should I capture R49-2 and R49-3 too? The schema is singular. Some might add \"related_recommendations\" — extra field. I'll add them as extra? Rule: schema adherence preferred. The certificate attests conformity with R49-1, R49-2, R49-3. I could add an extra key \"related_recommendations\": [{\"id\": \"R49-2\", \"edition\": 2006}, {\"id\": \"R49-3\", \"edition\": 2006}]. I think that's valuable and honest. I'll include it.\n\ntest_reports:\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01\", \"pages\": 41, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\", \"annexes\": 14},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\", \"annexes\": 3},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\", \"annexes\": 4},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\", \"annexes\": 3},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-13200159-01\", \"pages\": 6, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\", \"annexes\": 1}\n]\n```\nThe NMi-13200159-01 (6 pages, 1 annex) is probably an addendum. Role: test_report? It's a report associated with the certificate. I'll keep type_evaluation_report for the four R49-numbered ones and \"test_report\" for NMi-13200159-01? Hmm. It says \"the conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated OIML Test Report(s)\". All listed are associated test reports. I'll mark all as \"type_evaluation_report\"... or the last one as \"test_report\". The schema roles: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. I'll use type_evaluation_report for the R49-numbered ones and test_report for NMi-13200159-01. Eh — actually keep them all type_evaluation_report for consistency? The NMi-13200159-01 (6 pages, 1 annex) is likely an additional test report. I'll use \"test_report\" for it to distinguish. Fine.\n\ndate for reports: not given. Omit date field entirely.\n\nrevision_history:\n```json\n[\n  {\"revision\": \"Initial\", \"date\": \"2012-03-28\", \"changes\": null},\n  {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2012-10-22\", \"changes\": \"New software version added\"},\n  {\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2013-04-16\", \"changes\": \"New software version added\"},\n  {\"revision\": \"3\", \"date\": \"2014-09-26\", \"changes\": \"New software version added\"},\n  {\"revision\": \"4\", \"date\": \"2015-03-17\", \"changes\": \"Added sizes\"}\n]\n```\nChanges \"-\" for Initial → null.\n\ncertified_type description: combine both descriptions. \n\nOne more thing: \"Min/max admissible temperature (°C): 0,1/50\" — value min 0.1 max 50. Note: min admissible temperature 0.1°C. OK.\n\nNow, \"model_family.family_name\": \"WATERFLUX 3070\".\n\nDouble-check the models array count = 36 and consistent naming.\n\nLet me also make sure JSON has no duplicate keys and is parseable. I'll write it carefully.\n\nFinal structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {...},\n  \"issuing_authority\": {...},\n  \"applicants\": [...],\n  \"manufacturers\": [...],\n  \"certified_type\": {...},\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {...},\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...]\n}\n```\n\nSchema order suggests components before characteristics; we omit components.\n\nWrite out model_level fully. This will be long; proceed.\n\nFor type_level, labels:\n- min_max_admissible_temperature\n- indicating_range\n- orientation\n- environmental_class\n- power_supply_type\n- battery_voltage\n- battery_type\n- measurement_principle\n- flow_directions\n- straight_inlet_outlet_length\n- flow_conditioner\n- software_versions\n\nEach with {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}.\n\nFor straight_inlet_outlet_length: value {min: 0, max: null}, unit_symbol \"D\". OK.\n\nLet me now produce the JSON. I must be meticulous with the 36-element arrays keeping the model order:\n\nOrder (36):\n1 DN25-Q3=10-class2\n2 DN25-Q3=16-class2\n3 DN40-Q3=25-class2\n4 DN40-Q3=40-class2\n5 DN50-Q3=40-class2\n6 DN50-Q3=63-class2\n7 DN65-Q3=63-class2\n8 DN65-Q3=100-class2\n9 DN65-Q3=100-class1\n10 DN80-Q3=100-class2\n11 DN80-Q3=160-class2\n12 DN80-Q3=100-class1\n13 DN80-Q3=160-class1\n14 DN100-Q3=160-class2\n15 DN100-Q3=250-class2\n16 DN100-Q3=160-class1\n17 DN100-Q3=250-class1\n18 DN125-Q3=250-class2\n19 DN125-Q3=400-class2\n20 DN125-Q3=250-class1\n21 DN125-Q3=400-class1\n22 DN150-Q3=400-class2\n23 DN150-Q3=630-class2\n24 DN150-Q3=400-class1\n25 DN150-Q3=630-class1\n26 DN200-Q3=630-class2\n27 DN200-Q3=630-class1\n28 DN250-Q3=1000-class2\n29 DN250-Q3=1000-class1\n30 DN300-Q3=1600-class2\n31 DN300-Q3=1600-class1\n32 DN350-Q3=2500-class1or2\n33 DN400-Q3=4000-class1or2\n34 DN450-Q3=4000-class1or2\n35 DN500-Q3=6300-class1or2\n36 DN600-Q3=6300-class1or2\n\nNow write everything. Ensure no markdown fences in output. Output raw JSON only.",
